# Post-restart consumer recovery

The actual current ownership migration was retained. New execution first observed
five failing fast tests and project succession failures in both the complete
checkout and an isolated governance copy. These were not scanner findings: the
project's appended ownership section had no successor and old tests still expected
native OSV. Initial failing logs remain in .local/scanner-recovery-20261107/.

The project predecessor is the exact scanner-remediation project state. The new
registry pins the current append and review by digest; all old bytes remain in
before/. Unknown bytes, active downgrades, corrupted/missing registries, reviews,
preimages and symlink escapes are denied. Partial-copy fixtures now carry the
successor. The old native OSV success test now checks refusal even with a local
executable, proving that it never ran and no local database was created. A separate
missing native malware-tool test still checks absence without installation.

Observed verification: 112 fast cases, 107 passed and five real Docker cases
skipped; four focused succession cases passed including full-copy recovery and
adversarial mutations; maintenance reports 485 preserved governed files and
439/439 reconstructed inputs. Standalone script tests: 30 cases, 25 passed and
five Docker skips (overlapping coverage, not additive). The installed toolbox's
read-only validate accepts the consumer config; all eight external contract files
still match the imported records. No dev_audit writes, Docker runs, installs or
commits occurred in this recovery.

Source hashes and logs are recorded in this directory. Task closure wording was
reexamined after tests: it records the observations above and does not change
executable behavior, requirements, policy or selected tool versions. The changed
feature contract bindings receive new recovery observations; older observations
are retained verbatim in historical_evidence rather than rehashed. Cleanup was
not rerun: cleanup-reexamination.json compares the current RustSec-only registry,
recomputed RustSec identity and absence of the previously removed directories.

Current toolchain AC-01..04 remain INCONCLUSIVE; AC-05 and AC-06 retain bounded
local consumer acceptance, and AC-07 remains UNVERIFIED. Current Docker integration
requires the external owner's coordinated execution and cannot be inferred from
prior native or pre-migration runs. Full is not approved, and this recovery does
not waive 165 license-policy diagnostics, 12 duplicate families, RustSec warnings,
retained detection fixtures or the two incomplete Geiger member reports.
